    <p>Electrical work has always moved on word of mouth, and a panel that's kept a family safe for ten years advertises better than anything you could buy. But much of Cullman's best electrical work now comes from people with nobody to ask. The second-home owner who just closed on a Smith Lake place, with a dock that needs its wiring checked before summer, doesn't have a Cullman electrician's number — they search. The family that relocated to the county for the land and sat through their first rural outage has no one to call about a generator, so they type it into Google. The buyer who found a Federal Pacific panel behind the cover plate of an older place is looking online too. Those newcomers are increasingly where the dock, generator, and panel jobs originate, and not one of them crosses your referral chain — which is exactly <a href="/articles/why-word-of-mouth-isnt-enough/">where a good reputation stops being enough</a> once the customers with money to spend are strangers to the county. The one thing that stands in front of them is a real page.</p>

